From eb79af013caa0ae14231f7f8a4d6e524f785a344 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aquarat <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:31:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] avutil/hwcontext_v4l2request: request cacheable CAPTURE
 buffers

Decoded frames are read back by the CPU in transfer_data_from() (and by
hwdownload / map_from). The CAPTURE buffers are plain V4L2 MMAP buffers,
which drivers built on videobuf2's dma-contig allocator hand out as
coherent memory: on ARM SoCs without dma-coherent that is a Normal-NC
(uncached) mapping, where every load is a serialised DRAM transaction.
On an Apple M1 the copy of a 2304x1296 NV12 frame from such a mapping
runs at ~250 MB/s whatever the copy loop does (memcpy, NEON ld1x4,
ldnp), i.e. ~18 ms per frame, so the "hardware" decode ends up costing
4-5x the CPU time of software decode.

V4L2 has a standard opt-in for cacheable buffers: when the queue reports
V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_MMAP_CACHE_HINTS, V4L2_MEMORY_FLAG_NON_COHERENT in
VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS makes videobuf2 allocate non-coherent memory, map it
cacheable and do the cache maintenance itself at QBUF (clean) and DQBUF
(invalidate). Request it for the CAPTURE queue when the capability is
present; drivers that do not report it ignore the flag, so nothing
changes for them.

With the apple-avd driver setting allow_cache_hints, the same copy runs
at 18-22 GB/s (cached memcpy speed), and
  ffmpeg -hwaccel v4l2request -i 2304x1296.h264 -vf fps=5,scale=640:360
         -f rawvideo -pix_fmt yuv420p -
for 150 frames goes from 2.97 s user + 0.09 s sys to 0.36-0.40 s user +
0.11-0.14 s sys (software decode: 0.53 s user single-threaded), with
bit-identical output; decode + read-back alone ("-f null -") from
2.75 s user to 0.08 s user + 0.12 s sys.

Signed-off-by: <fill in>
---
 libavutil/hwcontext_v4l2request.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libavutil/hwcontext_v4l2request.c b/libavutil/hwcontext_v4l2request.c
index b6633d9..6876ed0 100644
--- a/libavutil/hwcontext_v4l2request.c
+++ b/libavutil/hwcontext_v4l2request.c
@@ -733,6 +733,21 @@ static AVBufferRef *v4l2request_v4l2_buffer_alloc(AVHWFramesContext *hwfc,
     uint8_t num_planes;
     AVBufferRef *ref;
 
+#ifdef V4L2_MEMORY_FLAG_NON_COHERENT
+    /*
+     * Ask for cacheable (non-coherent) CAPTURE buffers when the driver
+     * supports cache hints. The decoded frames are read back by the CPU
+     * (transfer_data_from / hwdownload) and on ARM SoCs the default
+     * coherent MMAP buffers are mapped non-cacheable, so every read goes
+     * uncached to DRAM (~250 MB/s on Apple M1). With non-coherent memory
+     * videobuf2 does the cache maintenance at QBUF/DQBUF and the mapping
+     * is cacheable, so the read-back runs at normal memcpy speed.
+     */
+    if (format->type == fctxi->capture.format.type &&
+        (fctxi->capture.capabilities & V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_MMAP_CACHE_HINTS))
+        buffers.flags = V4L2_MEMORY_FLAG_NON_COHERENT;
+#endif
+
     num_planes = V4L2_TYPE_IS_MULTIPLANAR(format->type) ?
                  format->fmt.pix_mp.num_planes : 0;
 
